<p>W.W. Wood Products has introduced a brand-new item: the <strong>Radius End Panel</strong>, delivered as a <strong>full lineup of Radius Columns</strong>. Each column puts a <strong>3″ radius</strong> on the end of a run — wall, base, vanity, utility, or peninsula — for the softened, furniture-style look designers have been asking for. Until now the usual answer was a custom shop.</p>

<div class="post-figure">
  <img src="/images/blog/radius-end-panel-columns/radius-end-panel-sample.webp" alt="Three views of a W.W. Wood radius end sample in white oak: a cutaway showing the plywood panel and solid wood quarter-round edge, and two finished views of the curved end" width="1400" height="1812" fetchpriority="high" />
</div>

<p><em>The production sample: a cutaway of the construction (left) and the finished curved end in white oak (right).</em></p>

<h2>How it works</h2>

<p>A Radius Column is a <strong>3″ wide</strong> piece with a <strong>3″ radius</strong> on its outside corner. You place it at the end of a cabinet run, in place of a square finished end, and order it as a <strong>Left</strong> or <strong>Right</strong> column depending on which end of the run it finishes.</p>

<p>The cutaway on the sample shows a plywood panel finished with a <strong>solid-wood quarter-round</strong> that forms the curve, so the radius is shaped wood rather than a bent or wrapped surface. On the finished faces, the grain carries around the curve with the rest of the panel.</p>

